_description:
`wp_validate_user_request_key()` reads `confirm_key` and `modified_timestamp` off the result of `wp_get_user_request()` before checking whether a request was found. `wp_get_user_request()` returns `false` when the ID is not a `user_request` post, so any confirmation link with a bogus `request_id` raises two PHP warnings:

{{{
E_WARNING: Attempt to read property "confirm_key" on false in wp-includes/user.php
E_WARNING: Attempt to read property "modified_timestamp" on false in wp-includes/user.php
}}}

The function still returns the correct `invalid_request` error, because `! $request` is checked on the next line, just too late. The result is log noise on any site that bots probe.

== Steps to reproduce ==

1. Visit `/wp-login.php?action=confirmaction&request_id=1&confirm_key=test` on a site where post 1 is not a `user_request` (e.g. the default "Hello world!" post).
2. Observe "Invalid personal data request." on screen and the two warnings in the error log.

This also happens with a non-existent ID, or when a plugin calls `wp_validate_user_request_key()` directly with an unvalidated ID.

== Suggested fix ==

Return `invalid_request` as soon as `wp_get_user_request()` returns `false`, then read the properties:

{{{#!php
<?php
$request_id = absint( $request_id );
$request    = wp_get_user_request( $request_id );

if ( ! $request ) {
	return new WP_Error( 'invalid_request', __( 'Invalid personal data request.' ) );
}

$saved_key        = $request->confirm_key;
$key_request_time = $request->modified_timestamp;

if ( ! $saved_key || ! $key_request_time ) {
	return new WP_Error( 'invalid_request', __( 'Invalid personal data request.' ) );
}
}}}

A patch with a unit test (regular post ID and a non-existent ID both return `invalid_request`) will follow.

The Installation, Upgrade, Upgrade Develop Version and Performance workflows install WP-CLI through the `tools` input of `shivammathur/setup-php`. That downloads the phar with a single unauthenticated `curl -sL` ([https://github.com/shivammathur/setup-php/blob/f3e473d116dcccaddc5834248c87452386958240/src/scripts/unix.sh#L147-L150 unix.sh]) and on failure only logs `✗ wp-cli Could not setup wp-cli`, leaving the step green ([https://github.com/shivammathur/setup-php/blob/f3e473d116dcccaddc5834248c87452386958240/src/scripts/tools/add_tools.sh#L238-L250 add_tools.sh]).

Jobs then fail on the first `wp` command with `wp: command not found` (exit 127). The HTTP status is discarded, so the logs don't show the cause. Failures come in bursts, e.g. 22 of 24 "Upgrade from" jobs in [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/actions/runs/38059129117/job/114234320838 run 38059129117]. Other examples: [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/actions/runs/38055702872/job/114223669953 38055702872], [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/actions/runs/39166479056/job/117790654883 39166479056].

Proposed fix: install WP-CLI through a shared `.github/actions/setup-wp-cli` composite action that:

 * caches the phar with `actions/cache` in 6 hour windows, so jobs keep getting the latest WP-CLI without each downloading it;
 * on a cache miss, downloads it with `curl --fail --retry 5 --retry-all-errors`;
 * if the download keeps failing, uses the previous window's phar with a warning, or fails the step with an error when there is none;
 * keeps WP-CLI 2.4.0 for the PHP 5.4/5.5 install jobs.

[https://core.trac.wordpress.org/attachment/ticket/66202/66202.patch 66202.patch] on #66202 replaces the hardcoded {{{$display_major_version}}} with the running version from {{{wp_get_wp_version()}}}. That ticket is the hub for the 7.2 About page, so this note is separate. The patch should not be applied.

{{{about.php}}} already has two values. The H1 uses the running version. {{{$display_major_version}}} is the release the page describes, and the welcome heading, release notes, field guide, and "See everything new" link all use it. It is bumped in the same commit as the copy, images, and documentation links. See [https://core.trac.wordpress.org/changeset/63182 r63182] for the 7.1 update.

Deriving it from the running version points those links at the patch number. For {{{7.1.3}}}, {{{sanitize_title()}}} produces {{{version-7-1-3}}}, {{{wordpress-7-1-3-field-guide}}}, and {{{download/releases/7-1-3/}}}. The [https://wordpress.org/documentation/wordpress-version/version-7-1-3/ 7.1.3 release notes] exist, and the 7.1.3 About page already links them from the "Maintenance and Security Releases" section. The field guide and the feature page do not exist for a minor: [https://make.wordpress.org/core/wordpress-7-1-3-field-guide/ wordpress-7-1-3-field-guide] and [https://wordpress.org/download/releases/7-1-3/ /download/releases/7-1-3/] return 404. The 7.1 pages do: [https://make.wordpress.org/core/wordpress-7-1-field-guide/ wordpress-7-1-field-guide] and [https://wordpress.org/download/releases/7-1/ /download/releases/7-1/].

Showing "Beta 1" or "RC 2" on the welcome heading would also need new translated strings, for a label that only appears between the About page commit and the stable release. The current {{{__( 'Welcome to WordPress %s' )}}} string was made reusable in #65895.

== Proposed change ==

Leave the version handling as it is. Comment {{{$display_major_version}}} so the next patch does not treat the hardcode as stale:

{{{
// Release this page describes. Bump this in the same commit as the copy, images,
// and documentation links. Prerelease and minor versions keep this value.
$display_major_version = '7.1';
}}}

When a single meta key has nothing stored, sending `null` for it (which means "reset") fails with a 500 `rest_invalid_stored_value` error if the key's type or schema doesn't accept an empty string. This affects `boolean`, `integer` and `enum` keys. Nothing is stored, so there is nothing to protect, but the request is rejected as if the stored value were invalid.

This also affects a core key: `_wp_note_status` on comments (enum `resolved`/`reopen`, no default) is returned as `null` when unset. Any client that sends the comment's meta back as it received it gets a 500.

=== Cause

In `WP_REST_Meta_Fields::update_value()`, a `null` value for a single key first validates the current value, so that invalid stored data isn't silently deleted:

{{{#!php
$current = get_metadata( $this->get_meta_type(), $object_id, $meta_key, true );

if ( is_wp_error( rest_validate_value_from_schema( $current, $args['schema'] ) ) ) {
        // rest_invalid_stored_value, 500
}
}}}

When no row exists, `get_metadata()` returns `''`, which fails validation for `boolean`, `integer` and `enum` schemas. So the check treats "nothing stored" as "invalid value stored".

=== Steps to reproduce

Tested on trunk (7.2-beta1), as an administrator, with no `auth_callback`.

 1. Register the keys:
{{{#!php
add_action( 'init', function () {
        register_post_meta( 'post', 'repro_flag', array( 'type' => 'boolean', 'single' => true, 'show_in_rest' => true ) );
        register_post_meta( 'post', 'repro_count', array( 'type' => 'integer', 'single' => true, 'show_in_rest' => true ) );
        register_post_meta( 'post', 'repro_choice', array(
                'type'         => 'string',
                'single'       => true,
                'show_in_rest' => array( 'schema' => array( 'type' => 'string', 'enum' => array( 'a', 'b' ) ) ),
        ) );
        register_post_meta( 'post', 'repro_text', array( 'type' => 'string', 'single' => true, 'show_in_rest' => true ) );
} );
}}}
 2. Create a post without setting any of these keys.
 3. For each key, `POST /wp/v2/posts/<id>` with `{"meta":{"<key>":null}}`.

|| Key (nothing stored) || GET returns || POST `null` ||
|| `repro_flag` (boolean) || `false` || 500 `rest_invalid_stored_value` ||
|| `repro_count` (integer) || `0` || 500 `rest_invalid_stored_value` ||
|| `repro_choice` (enum) || `null` || 500 `rest_invalid_stored_value` ||
|| `repro_text` (string) || `""` || 200 ||

Comments, with only core meta registered:

 1. Create a comment.
 2. `GET /wp/v2/comments/<id>?context=edit` returns `"meta": {"_wp_note_status": null}`.
 3. `POST /wp/v2/comments/<id>` with an edited `content` and the `meta` exactly as returned.
 4. The response is 500 `rest_invalid_stored_value` for `_wp_note_status`, but the edited content has already been saved.

=== Expected

If nothing is stored, a `null` reset is a no-op and should succeed. The `rest_invalid_stored_value` protection only makes sense when there is a stored value.

=== Possible fix

Check for a stored row before validating, for example with `get_metadata_raw()`, which returns `null` when no row exists:

{{{#!php
$current = get_metadata_raw( $this->get_meta_type(), $object_id, $meta_key, true );

if ( null !== $current && is_wp_error( rest_validate_value_from_schema( $current, $args['schema'] ) ) ) {
}}}

A stored empty string that fails the schema would still be protected.

=== Related

 * #66329 fixes one way to hit this (revisions returning non-revisioned meta, e.g. `wp_pattern_sync_status`), but not the underlying check.
 * #48426 / PR 14328 moves the "nothing stored" early return in `delete_meta_value()` before the authorization check. With this fix, a `null` for an unset protected key would reach that path.
 * #64019 rewords the `rest_invalid_stored_value` messages.

=== Description ===

`wp.editor.autop()` in `wp-admin/js/editor.js` has special handling for `[caption]` shortcodes: existing `<br>` tags are protected as `<wp-temp-br>`, and then '''every''' remaining line break inside the caption is converted to another `<wp-temp-br />`:

{{{
// Keep <br> tags inside captions.
if ( text.indexOf( '[caption' ) !== -1 ) {
    preserve_br = true;
    text = text.replace( /\[caption[\s\S]+?\[\/caption\]/g, function( a ) {
        a = a.replace( /<br([^>]*)>/g, '<wp-temp-br$1>' );
        a = a.replace( /<[^<>]+>/g, function( b ) {
            return b.replace( /[\n\t ]+/, ' ' );
        });
        return a.replace( /\s*\n\s*/g, '<wp-temp-br />' );
    });
}
}}}

A caption that contains `<br />` immediately followed by a newline (`<br />\n`) therefore becomes `<br /><br />`. The PHP counterpart `wpautop()` guards against exactly this case (`wp-includes/formatting.php`):

{{{
$text = preg_replace( '|(?<!<br />)\s*\n|', "<br />\n", $text );
}}}

So the two implementations disagree, and the JS version is not idempotent: `autop( autop( x ) ) !== autop( x )` for such captions.

`<br />\n` inside a caption is a natural form of the Text-tab markup. Users write it by hand, and some editor plugins produce it on save (e.g. Advanced Editor Tools with "Keep paragraph tags" enabled adds a newline after every `<br>`; combined with this bug the number of `<br />` tags in a caption doubles on every Text → Visual switch, growing to hundreds).

=== Environment info ===

 * WordPress 6.9.10, Classic Editor 1.7.0 (no other plugins needed), default theme
 * TinyMCE 4.9.11 bundled with core
 * Reproduced in Chrome (macOS)

=== Steps to reproduce ===

 1. Create a post with the Classic Editor and switch to the '''Text''' tab.
 2. Paste the following (use the attachment ID/URL of any image in the media library):
{{{
[caption id="attachment_1" align="alignnone" width="300"]<img src="https://example.test/wp-content/uploads/image.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="200" class="wp-image-1 size-medium" /> Line one<br />
Line two<br />
Line three[/caption]
}}}
 3. Switch to the '''Visual''' tab.
 4. Switch back to the '''Text''' tab.

=== Expected result ===

The caption still contains two `<br />` tags (one per line break).

=== Actual result ===

The caption contains four `<br />` tags: `Line one<br /><br />Line two<br /><br />Line three`. Each existing `<br />` followed by a newline has been doubled.

The same can be observed directly in the browser console:

{{{
wp.editor.autop( '[caption id="attachment_1" width="300"]<img src="x.jpg" width="300" height="200" /> A<br />\nB[/caption]' )
// => ... A<br /><br />B ...
}}}

=== Suggested fix ===

Drop the newline that directly follows a protected `<br>` before converting the remaining newlines, mirroring the PHP lookbehind:

{{{
a = a.replace( /<br([^>]*)>/g, '<wp-temp-br$1>' );
a = a.replace( /(<wp-temp-br[^>]*>)\s*\n\s*/g, '$1' );
a = a.replace( /<[^<>]+>/g, function( b ) {
    return b.replace( /[\n\t ]+/, ' ' );
});
return a.replace( /\s*\n\s*/g, '<wp-temp-br />' );
}}}

This makes `wp.editor.autop()` idempotent for captions and consistent with `wpautop()`.

The `serialize_block_attributes()` function encodes the attributes array as-is, so an array that PHP sees as a list is serialized as a JSON array rather than an object. The block delimiter grammar only accepts an object, so a block whose attributes come out this way is no longer a block when its content is parsed again.

This happens when a block's attribute names are numeric. The `parse_blocks()` function decodes the delimiter's JSON into associative arrays, and PHP turns numeric-string keys into integer keys, so `{"0":"a"}` decodes to `array( 0 => 'a' )`, which `wp_json_encode()` then encodes as `["a"]`:

{{{#!php
<?php
$serialized = serialize_blocks( parse_blocks( '<!-- wp:paragraph {"0":"a"} /-->' ) );
// <!-- wp:paragraph ["a"] /-->

$reparsed = parse_blocks( $serialized );
// $reparsed[0]['blockName'] is null: the paragraph is now freeform content.
}}}

The same happens for `{"0":"a","1":"b"}`, which becomes `["a","b"]`. Numeric names that do not count up from zero, such as `{"1":"a"}`, are not affected, since PHP does not see them as a list.

Called directly, the function also returns:

 * `[]` for an empty array, rather than `{}`. Core's callers check `empty()` first, so they are not affected.
 * An empty string when the attributes cannot be encoded, such as when a value is `INF`: `wp_json_encode()` returns `false`, which `strtr()` turns into `''`. That is not JSON, and the JavaScript `serializeAttributes()` never returns it. The block grammar happens to tolerate it, since core's callers surround the attributes with spaces, so a paragraph with `'align' => 'wide'` and `'ratio' => INF` is serialized as `<!-- wp:paragraph  /-->`. It is still parsed as a paragraph, but with none of its attributes, not even `align`.

Besides a round trip through `parse_blocks()` and `serialize_blocks()`, block delimiters are re-serialized through this function by `wp_strip_custom_css_from_blocks()`, and by the block delimiter normalization in `wp_sanitize_html_kses()`, which `wp_kses()` uses when the `wp_kses_force_legacy_parser` filter, new in 7.2, returns false.

The docblock of `serialize_block_attributes()` says its output must remain in sync with the JavaScript `serializeAttributes()` in the block editor. That function takes an attributes object (`Record< string, unknown >`), which `JSON.stringify()` always encodes as `{…}`.

Nested attribute values have a similar problem, which is out of scope here: since the delimiter's JSON is decoded into associative arrays, a nested `{}` or `{"0":"x"}` is re-serialized as `[]` or `["x"]`.

Related: #65817

🤖 ''Ticket drafted with support from Claude Opus 5.5.''

When `json_encode()` fails, for example on a string that is not valid UTF-8, `wp_json_encode()` retries with the data passed through `_wp_json_sanity_check()`, which converts strings to UTF-8. That function does not use `JsonSerializable::jsonSerialize()`. It rebuilds each object from its public properties instead. An object that keeps its data in private properties is then encoded as `{}`.

{{{#!php
<?php
class Example implements JsonSerializable {
	private $data = array( 'kind' => 'serialized' );

	public function jsonSerialize(): array {
		return $this->data;
	}
}

echo wp_json_encode( array( 'object' => new Example() ) ), "\n";
echo wp_json_encode(
	array(
		'object' => new Example(),
		'text'   => "caf\xE9", // Not valid UTF-8.
	)
), "\n";
}}}

Output on trunk:

{{{
{"object":{"kind":"serialized"}}
{"object":{},"text":"caf?"}
}}}

In core, this affects `WP_Speculation_Rules`, whose rules are in a private property, and `WP_Secret`, which serializes to a mask. `WP_Interactivity_Dangerous_HTML` works around it with `getIterator()` ([64501], #66246), and `WP_Interactivity_API` documents the current behavior where it resolves bound objects (#65740).

The fallback has worked this way since `wp_json_encode()` was added in [30055]. #37856 looked at the same code and was closed in favor of #47699.

Proposed change: in the retry, use the value each `JsonSerializable` object serializes to, as `json_encode()` does, with its strings converted to UTF-8. Rebuild an object from its public properties, as now, only when its serialized value still cannot be encoded: it holds `INF`, `NAN` or a resource, its `jsonSerialize()` throws, or objects serialize to each other in a cycle. When the retry still fails as a whole, for example when the data is deeper than `$depth`, run the current fallback unchanged. Data that `json_encode()` encodes on the first try is not affected.

When the Media Library grid uses the "Load more" button, it shows how many media items are loaded:

{{{#!js
this.loadMoreCount.$el.text(
	/* translators: 1: Number of displayed attachments, 2: Number of total attachments. */
	sprintf(
		__( 'Showing %1$s of %2$s media items' ),
		this.collection.length,
		this.collection.getTotalAttachments()
	)
);
}}}

This is `updateLoadMoreView()` in `src/js/media/views/attachments/browser.js` (line 611 on trunk), added in [50829] for #50105.

There are two problems:

 1. The string has no plural form, so a library with one media item shows "Showing 1 of 1 media items", and translators get one form for every count.
 2. The translators comment sits above `sprintf()` instead of the `__()` call, so it is not extracted. `wp i18n make-pot` on trunk's `browser.js` gives the string without a `#.` line, and the wp/dev Hindi export from translate.wordpress.org has no comment for it either.

Since [62632] (#65564), infinite scrolling is on by default, so this count only shows for users who turn it off in their profile or on sites that filter `media_library_infinite_scrolling` to false.

=== Steps to reproduce ===

 1. On a site with exactly one media item, go to Users > Profile, check "Disable infinite scrolling in the Media Library grid view" and save.
 2. Go to Media > Library in grid view.
 3. Below the item, the count reads "Showing 1 of 1 media items".

Tested on WordPress 7.1.3, whose `updateLoadMoreView()` is identical to trunk.

=== Suggested fix ===

Use `_n()`, with the plural form following the total, and put the comment directly above the `_n()` call:

{{{#!js
this.loadMoreCount.$el.text(
	sprintf(
		/* translators: 1: Number of displayed attachments, 2: Number of total attachments. */
		_n(
			'Showing %1$s of %2$s media item',
			'Showing %1$s of %2$s media items',
			this.collection.getTotalAttachments()
		),
		this.collection.length,
		this.collection.getTotalAttachments()
	)
);
}}}

With this, `make-pot` extracts the comment and both forms, from the source file and from the built `wp-includes/js/media-views.js`. Existing translations of the old string do not carry over to the new pair.

Related: the open count tickets (#57488, #58729, #65513) are about the numbers, not the wording. [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/12392 PR 12392] for #65513 adds an e2e assertion for "Showing 1 of 1 media items", which this change would turn into "Showing 1 of 1 media item".

Originally reported in Gutenberg: https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/issues/80678 | Reported by @mcliwanow

=== Problem ===

Two editor sessions can disagree about whether a note still exists. When one session deletes a note while another still has it open, the second session can keep changing it, and the REST API accepts every change:

 * '''Replying''' to the deleted note succeeds and the editor announces "Reply added.", but the reply is stored under the trashed note and is no longer shown anywhere in the editor.
 * '''Reopen & Reply''' on a resolved note sets the note's status, which moves it out of the trash without the replies that were trashed along with it, so the thread comes back with only the new reply.
 * Resolving or editing a deleted note is accepted in the same way.

This is distinct from #64240, which trashes the replies that already exist when a top-level note is trashed. Here, the change arrives after the delete has completed.

`WP_REST_Comments_Controller::create_item()` already rejects a reaction whose parent note is trashed or spam ("A reaction cannot be added to a trashed or spam note."), but not a note reply. `update_item()` changes the status or content of a trashed note like any other comment.

=== Steps to reproduce ===

In the editor:

 1. Sign the same administrator into two browser sessions, A and B (for example a normal and a private window).
 2. In A, create a post, add a Paragraph, add a note to it, and save the draft.
 3. Open the same post in B and open the note.
 4. In A, select the note and type a reply, but don't submit it.
 5. In B, delete the note from its Actions menu and wait for "Note deleted.".
 6. In A, without reloading, click Reply.

Result: "Reply added." After a reload, the reply is not shown, but it is stored with the deleted note as its parent.

For Reopen & Reply, resolve the note in A before step 4. The button then reads "Reopen & Reply", and clicking it brings back the deleted note as open, while its earlier replies stay in the trash.

The same thing from the browser console, in a single session, on a saved draft in the editor:

{{{#!js
( async () => {
	const post = wp.data.select( 'core/editor' ).getCurrentPostId();
	const api = ( path, method, data ) => wp.apiFetch( { path, method, data } );

	const note = await api( '/wp/v2/comments', 'POST', { post, content: 'Note', status: 'hold', type: 'note' } );
	await api( `/wp/v2/comments/${ note.id }`, 'DELETE' ); // Moves the note to the trash.

	// 1. A reply to the deleted note is accepted.
	const reply = await api( '/wp/v2/comments', 'POST', { post, content: 'Reply', status: 'hold', type: 'note', parent: note.id } );
	console.log( 'Reply stored under trashed note:', reply.id, 'parent', reply.parent );

	// 2. Reopening the deleted note moves it out of the trash.
	const reopened = await api( `/wp/v2/comments/${ note.id }`, 'POST', { status: 'hold' } );
	console.log( 'Deleted note is now:', reopened.status );
} )();
}}}

=== Expected ===

 * Creating a note reply whose parent is trashed, spam, or no longer exists returns an error (for example `400 rest_comment_invalid_parent`), as it already does for reactions.
 * Any change to a trashed or spam note other than restoring it (`status: untrash` or `unspam`) returns an error, so the editor can tell the user instead of losing their change. Undo in the editor uses `untrash`, so it keeps working.

The editor already shows REST error messages in a snackbar and keeps the typed text when a save fails, so no client changes are needed.

=== Fix ===

A fix for the Gutenberg plugin's WordPress 7.2 compat controller, with tests, is in https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/pull/85202. A Core patch with the same changes and PHPUnit tests will follow on this ticket. Suggesting the 7.2 milestone, since 7.2 also adds emoji reactions to notes in the same controller.

In `wp_get_shortlink()` with the `query` context, `$post_id` is taken from `get_queried_object_id()` while `$post` is taken from `get_post( $post_id )`. When the queried object ID is non-zero but `get_post()` returns `null`, the `! empty( $post_id )` branch still runs and reads `$post->post_type`:

{{{
E_WARNING: Attempt to read property "post_type" on null in wp-includes/link-template.php
}}}

This is reached through `wp_shortlink_header()` on `template_redirect` (and `wp_shortlink_wp_head()`).

We saw this on WordPress.org from a scanner that POSTed a large number of junk parameters to a handbook landing page (a singular page whose query is adjusted in `pre_get_posts`). In that case `is_singular()` is true and the queried object ID is set, but no post is found for it.

The `post` context branch already guards against this by only setting `$post_id` from `$post->ID`. The proposed fix does the same for the `query` context:

{{{#!php
<?php
	if ( 'query' === $context && is_singular() ) {
		$post = get_post( get_queried_object_id() );
		if ( $post ) {
			$post_id = $post->ID;
		}
	}
}}}

PR: https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/14543

Related: #51376 (a different notice in the same function).

On multisite, `WP_Plugin_Dependencies::check_plugin_dependencies_during_ajax()` reads `$status['activateUrl']` without checking that it is set.

The method sets `activateUrl` only when the current user can activate the plugin and the plugin is inactive:

{{{#!php
<?php
if ( current_user_can( 'activate_plugin', $plugin_file ) && is_plugin_inactive( $plugin_file ) ) {
        $status['activateUrl'] = add_query_arg( /* ... */ );
}

if ( is_multisite() && current_user_can( 'manage_network_plugins' ) ) {
        $status['activateUrl'] = add_query_arg( array( 'networkwide' => 1 ), $status['activateUrl'] );
}
}}}

The second block runs for every super admin. When the plugin is already active, on the current site or network-wide, `activateUrl` was never set.


== Steps to reproduce

1. Use a multisite install and log in as a super admin.
2. Network-activate any plugin, for example `hello-dolly/hello.php`.
3. Send a `check_plugin_dependencies` AJAX request with `slug=hello-dolly` and a valid `updates` nonce.
4. The PHP log shows `Undefined array key "activateUrl"`, and the response contains `"activateUrl":"?networkwide=1"`.

Expected: no warning, and no `activateUrl` in the response. Single-site already behaves this way for active plugins.


The code was introduced in [57545] with Plugin Dependencies. See #22316.

Follow-up to #65089.

== Background

Since #65089, the default sidebar menu and the admin bar icons are rendered as inline SVGs via `wp_get_icon()`. These icons come from the non-public `core-admin` collection, which was introduced in #66114.

Right now, any plugin or theme can remove these icons:

{{{#!php
<?php
add_action( 'init', function () {
	wp_unregister_icon_collection( 'core-admin' );
}, 20 );

// Or a single icon:
add_action( 'init', function () {
	wp_unregister_icon( 'core-admin/dashboard' );
}, 20 );
}}}

When that happens, the admin menu and admin bar render without icons.

Note that the `core` collection is a different case. It serves the Icon block and the editor's icon picker, and unregistering it there is a valid use case. The admin screens use `core-admin` precisely so that it does not depend on `core`.

== Proposal

Make `core-admin` and its icons non-unregisterable, following the precedent of  [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/213ecca2e96f277cb4557e773a492821f9a630ea/src/wp-includes/functions.wp-scripts.php#L367-L423 wp_deregister_script()], which refuses to deregister core scripts such as `jquery` in the admin screens:


{{{#!php
<?php
function wp_deregister_script( $handle ) {
    ...
		if ( in_array( $handle, $not_allowed, true ) ) {
			_doing_it_wrong(
				__FUNCTION__,
				sprintf(
					/* translators: 1: Script name, 2: wp_enqueue_scripts */
					__( 'Do not deregister the %1$s script in the administration area. To target the front-end theme, use the %2$s hook.' ),
					"<code>$handle</code>",
					'<code>wp_enqueue_scripts</code>'
				),
				'3.6.0'
			);
			return;
		}
	}

	wp_scripts()->remove( $handle );
}
}}}


== Context

Original discussion from @t-hamano: https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/12270#pullrequestreview-5547068216

The menu item quick search on Appearance > Menus tells screen reader users how many results it found with:

{{{#!js
wp.a11y.speak( wp.i18n.sprintf( wp.i18n.__( '%d Search Results Found' ), $items.length ), 'assertive' );
}}}

This is in `src/js/_enqueues/lib/nav-menu.js` (line 1842 on trunk), added in [60991] for #48655.

The English string is always plural, so a search that finds one item announces "1 Search Results Found". The string also uses `__()` instead of `_n()` and has no translators comment, so translators cannot give the right form for each count. That matters most for languages with more than two plural forms.

When nothing matches, the same function returns earlier and announces "No results found.", so this line only runs for one result or more.

=== Steps to reproduce ===

 1. Use a theme with a menu location, or register one, and go to Appearance > Menus.
 2. In Add menu items, open Posts and select the Search tab.
 3. Search for a term that matches exactly one post.
 4. A screen reader announces "1 Search Results Found". The text is also visible in the `#a11y-speak-assertive` live region.

Tested on WordPress 7.1.3, whose quick search handler is identical to trunk.

=== Suggested fix ===

Use `_n()` with a translators comment, as `tags-suggest.js` does for its search results, and reuse the plural pair core already has:

{{{#!js
wp.a11y.speak(
	wp.i18n.sprintf(
		/* translators: %d: Number of search results found. */
		wp.i18n._n( '%d result found.', '%d results found.', $items.length ),
		$items.length
	),
	'assertive'
);
}}}

The pair `%d result found.` / `%d results found.` is already in `wordpress.pot`, used by the block editor. `nav-menu.js` already gets another string from that catalog the same way: "No results found." is in `wordpress.pot` with a `wp-admin/js/nav-menu.js` reference, and the Hindi language pack's JSON file for `nav-menu.js` includes its translation. So existing translations of the pair should reach the Menus screen once language packs are rebuilt with the new reference. The new wording also matches that "No results found." message.

Gutenberg stabilizes the DataForm-based editor inspector in [https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/pull/85081 #85081], so the document sidebar of the post editor always renders the DataForm post summary.

The post summary makes different requests on load than the previous inspector, so the preload paths in `wp-admin/edit-form-blocks.php` need to be updated:

1. Add the form view config of the post type, which the summary requests through `useViewConfig`:
{{{
/wp/v2/view-config?kind=postType&name=<post_type>&_fields=form
}}}
2. Remove the author path, which was used by the old author panel and is not requested anymore:
{{{
/wp/v2/users/<id>?context=view&_fields=id,name
}}}

The minimal reproduction
{{{
sanitize_text_field( "I'm <3 & \"q\"" )  →  I'm &lt;3 &amp; &quot;q&quot;
sanitize_text_field( "I'm & \"q\"" )     →  I'm & "q"   (unchanged: no <)
}}}

The cause: 
{{{
_sanitize_text_fields() → wp_pre_kses_less_than() → wp_pre_kses_less_than_callback() → esc_html( $matches[0] ).
}}}

The behaviour comes from wp_pre_kses_less_than() (2.3) being reused by _sanitize_text_fields() (2.9); 7.1.3 is where it was observed.

The function's documentation (sanitize_text_field()) lists "Converts single < characters to entities" and says nothing about &, ' or " being encoded.

A scoped patch: give _sanitize_text_fields() its own callback that encodes only the < of a lone span, and leave wp_pre_kses_less_than() alone for its pre_kses use, where the input is HTML and escaping a non-tag span is correct (kses normalises & itself anyway). That keeps the documented behaviour exactly and drops only the collateral.

Where it bites: any plugin that emits stored text outside HTML such as plain-text mail, ICS, JSON to another system as well as the developer-facing surprise that the apostrophe two lines later is escaped because of a heart.

The Command Palette registers a command for every admin menu item (#63845), but these commands can only be found by their label. Searching for "favicon", "timezone" or "password" finds nothing, even though Settings > General and Users > Profile are where those are.

The Command Palette has supported keywords since [https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/pull/70624 Gutenberg #70624]. When [https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/pull/71476 Gutenberg #71476] moved admin navigation to commands generated from the admin menu, there was no longer a way to attach keywords to them. That was listed as one of its trade-offs.

The proposal is that keywords come from PHP, next to the `label`, `url` and `name` that `wp_enqueue_command_palette_assets()` already sends.


== Proposed change ==

 * A new function, `wp_get_admin_menu_command_keywords()`, returns keywords for core admin screens, keyed by menu slug.
 * A new filter, `admin_menu_command_keywords`, lets plugins add keywords for their own pages. They key them by the same `$menu_slug` they pass to `add_menu_page()` or `add_submenu_page()`.
 * `wp_enqueue_command_palette_assets()` adds a `keywords` list to each top-level and submenu command. Commands without keywords get an empty list.

For example, a plugin could add:

{{{
add_filter(
        'admin_menu_command_keywords',
        function ( $keywords ) {
                $keywords['my-shop'] = array( 'orders', 'products' );
                return $keywords;
        }
);
}}}

The Gutenberg side, which passes the keywords through to the palette, is in [https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/pull/84834 Gutenberg #84834].

On a multisite sub-site, wp-admin/plugin-install.php and wp-admin/theme-install.php redirect to their network admin counterparts but drop the query string. Any link to these screens with query args from a sub-site loses its search, tab, or preview.

This is visible on Settings > Connectors (wp-admin/options-connectors.php), where the "search the plugin directory" link opens the network Add Plugins screen without the search, so the connector results are not shown.

=== Steps to reproduce ===
 1. On a multisite install, log in as a super admin.
 2. Go to a sub-site's Settings > Connectors.
 3. Click "search the plugin directory" in the help text below the connectors list.

Expected: wp-admin/network/plugin-install.php?s=connector&tab=search&type=tag
Actual: wp-admin/network/plugin-install.php

On a single site the link works as expected.

The same happens with the Add Themes screen. As a super admin, open a sub-site URL such as:
 * /site2/wp-admin/theme-install.php?search=twenty
 * /site2/wp-admin/theme-install.php?theme=twentytwentyfive

Expected: wp-admin/network/theme-install.php?search=twenty (or ?theme=twentytwentyfive)
Actual: wp-admin/network/theme-install.php?browse=popular

The ?browse=popular is not the original query surviving the redirect. It is added by the theme installer's JS router, which falls back to the Popular tab when no query is present.

=== Cause ===
The Connectors link is relative (plugin-install.php?s=connector&tab=search&type=tag), so on a sub-site it resolves to the site's wp-admin/plugin-install.php. That file redirects to the network admin and drops the query string:

{{{
if ( is_multisite() && ! is_network_admin() ) {
	wp_redirect( network_admin_url( 'plugin-install.php' ) );
	exit;
}
}}}

wp-admin/theme-install.php has the same redirect:

{{{
if ( is_multisite() && ! is_network_admin() ) {
	wp_redirect( network_admin_url( 'theme-install.php' ) );
	exit;
}
}}}

=== Proposed fix ===
Keep the query string when redirecting in both wp-admin/plugin-install.php and wp-admin/theme-install.php. This fixes the Connectors link and any other link to these screens with query args from a sub-site, including links added by plugins and themes.

A duotone preset whose slug is not already kebab-case (for example {{{blueOrange}}} or {{{duotone1}}}) works in the block editor but is not applied on the front end.

{{{WP_Duotone}}} registers presets under a kebab-cased filter ID ({{{get_all_global_styles_presets()}}} uses {{{_wp_to_kebab_case( $preset['slug'] )}}}), but two other places use the raw slug:

* {{{get_slug_from_attribute()}}} returns the slug exactly as written in the block attribute, so the lookup in {{{is_preset()}}} fails.
* {{{get_global_styles_presets()}}} names the CSS custom property after the raw slug.

The block editor saves the reference with the raw slug ({{{var:preset|duotone|blueOrange}}}) and resolves the colors itself, which is why the filter shows correctly in the editor.

== Steps to reproduce

1. In a block theme's {{{theme.json}}}, add a duotone preset with a camelCase slug:
{{{
"settings": { "color": { "duotone": [
	{ "slug": "blueOrange", "name": "Blue Orange", "colors": [ "#0000ff", "#ffa500" ] }
] } }
}}}
2. Add an Image block to a post and apply the "Blue Orange" duotone filter. It shows in the editor.
3. Save and view the post on the front end.

== Actual result

The image has no filter. The preset is not recognised, so the attribute is treated as a raw CSS value:

{{{
<figure class="wp-block-image wp-duotone-varpresetduotoneblueorange-1">

.wp-duotone-varpresetduotoneblueorange-1.wp-block-image img { filter: var:preset|duotone|blueOrange; }
}}}

The declaration is invalid CSS and no SVG filter is printed.

The same preset applied through global styles ({{{styles.blocks.core/image.filter.duotone}}}) fails differently. Since [63641] (#66121) the reference is kebab-cased, so the block CSS and the SVG filter use {{{blue-orange}}}, but the custom property is still defined with the raw slug, so the reference never resolves:

{{{
:root { --wp--preset--duotone--blueOrange: url(#wp-duotone-blue-orange); }
.wp-duotone-blue-orange.wp-block-image img { filter: var(--wp--preset--duotone--blue-orange); }
}}}

== Expected result

The filter is applied on the front end, with the class name, SVG filter ID, custom property and block CSS all using the kebab-cased slug ({{{blue-orange}}}).

== Proposed fix

Convert the slug to kebab-case in {{{WP_Duotone::get_slug_from_attribute()}}} and in {{{WP_Duotone::get_global_styles_presets()}}}, so every part uses the same form the presets are registered under. Slugs that are already kebab-case are unchanged.

The block attribute path has behaved this way since the class was introduced in 6.3. I have a patch with unit tests and will open a PR shortly.

`wp_get_state_declarations_with_fallback_border_styles()` is documented to return an array:

{{{
/**
 * ...
 *
 * @param array $declarations CSS declarations generated by the style engine.
 * @return array CSS declarations with fallback border styles applied where needed.
 */
function wp_get_state_declarations_with_fallback_border_styles( $declarations ) { ... }
}}}

However, when a non-array argument is passed, the function returns that argument unchanged:
{{{
if ( ! is_array( $declarations ) ) {
    return $declarations;
}
}}}

The function can therefore return a value of any type, contrary to its documented return type.

I am unsure of the potential side effects, but the function should probably either return an empty array:
{{{
if ( ! is_array( $declarations ) ) {
    return array();
}
}}}

or set `$declarations` to an empty array so that the fallback logic is applied:
{{{
if ( ! is_array( $declarations ) ) {
    $declarations = array();
}
}}}

The same applies to `wp_get_state_style_with_fallback_dimension_styles()`.

When Ghostscript (`gs`) is not installed, running the "Media Handling" section of Site Health from the command line prints an error message coming from the shell:
{{{
sh: gs: not found
}}}
The "Ghostscript version" field is still correctly reported as "Not available", so nothing breaks. The output is only noisy.

== Cause ==
`WP_Debug_Data::get_wp_media()` runs `gs --version` through `exec()`:
{{{#!php
<?php
$gs = exec( 'gs --version' );
}}}

`exec()` only captures stdout. When `gs` does not exist, the shell (not PHP) writes `sh: gs: not found` to stderr, and a terminal shows it as is. The empty result is already handled just below (`empty( $gs )`), so the intent of the code is to report "Not available" quietly.
This probably goes unnoticed in a web request, where the stderr of the child process is not part of the page output.
This code has been there since this class was introduced in WordPress 5.2.

== Steps to reproduce ==
1. Use an environment where `gs` is not installed (`command -v gs` prints nothing).
2. Use a WP-CLI version that includes the `site-health` command. It is missing in WP-CLI 2.12.0 (stable), so I used the nightly build.
3. Run `wp site-health info wp-media`.
4. Observe `sh: gs: not found` printed above the table.

The same message appears in any command line context that calls `WP_Debug_Data::debug_data()`. I first noticed it while running the PHPUnit tests of a plugin.

'''Expected result:''' no output besides the table, and "Ghostscript version" shows "Not available".

'''Actual result:''' `sh: gs: not found` is printed on stderr.

Tested with: WordPress trunk (7.2.0 in `package.json`, commit c23817e), WP-CLI 3.0.0-alpha-dd11f5a (nightly), PHP 8.3.33, Docker (Linux), using the wordpress-develop local environment.

== Reproducing with PHPUnit ==
Save the following as `tests/phpunit/tests/admin/wpDebugData.php` and run `npm run test:php -- --filter Tests_Admin_wpDebugData`.
With the current code, the PHPUnit output contains `sh: 1: gs: not found` (the exact wording depends on the shell). With the proposed fix below, the output is clean and the test still passes.

{{{#!php
<?php
<?php
/**
 * @group admin
 * @group site-health
 */
class Tests_Admin_wpDebugData extends WP_UnitTestCase {

	public function test_debug_data_without_ghostscript() {
		require_once ABSPATH . 'wp-admin/includes/class-wp-debug-data.php';

		// Block the external request made by WP_Debug_Data.
		add_filter(
			'pre_http_request',
			function () {
				return new WP_Error( 'test_http_request', 'HTTP request disabled for this test.' );
			}
		);

		// Simulate "gs is not installed" with a PATH that does not contain it.
		$original_path = getenv( 'PATH' );
		putenv( 'PATH=/nonexistent' );

		try {
			$info = WP_Debug_Data::debug_data();
		} finally {
			putenv( 'PATH=' . $original_path );
		}

		// The test passes, but with the current code "sh: gs: not found" is printed.
		$this->assertSame(
			'not available',
			$info['wp-media']['fields']['ghostscript_version']['debug']
		);
	}
}
}}}


== Proposed fix ==

Check that `gs` exists before running it:


{{{#!php
<?php
$gs = exec( 'command -v gs >/dev/null && gs --version' );
}}}

`command -v` is a shell builtin specified by POSIX, whereas `which` is an external program that is not guaranteed to be installed. With this change, in my test environment, the message disappears and the field is still reported as "Not available".


== Notes ==

* The `wp-cli/site-health-command` package already works around this message in several of its acceptance tests (see the comments in `features/site-health.feature`: https://github.com/wp-cli/site-health-command/blob/main/features/site-health.feature), so other projects are affected too.
* As far as I can see, no existing core PHPUnit test covers this section of `WP_Debug_Data`. Since the message comes from a child process and not from PHP, I am not sure how to assert on it in an automated test. Suggestions are welcome.
* I will open a pull request with this change.
* Related: #61648 (where `get_wp_media()` was extracted; the `exec()` call itself is older and was only moved).

AI assistance: Claude (Anthropic) helped me understand the problem, look up the affected version and write this report.

Patch proposed by Chouby.

Follow-up to #64156 and #66224.

#64156 lets repeated `add_post_type_support()` calls merge their array arguments, and #66224 lets `remove_post_type_support()` remove individual sub-features. `post_type_supports()` still only answers whether the feature as a whole is supported.

To check whether a post type has `notes` enabled on the `editor` feature today, you have to reach into the raw arguments yourself:

{{{#!php
<?php
$supports = get_all_post_type_supports( 'custom' );
$has_notes = isset( $supports['editor'][0]['notes'] ) && $supports['editor'][0]['notes'];
}}}

This was suggested by @mamaduka on the #64156 PR.

== Proposal

Add an optional third parameter, `$sub_feature`:

{{{#!php
<?php
post_type_supports( 'custom', 'editor', 'notes' );
}}}

* For associative arguments, the sub-feature is supported when its key is set to a non-empty value, so `'notes' => false` counts as not supported.
* For list arguments (e.g. a `post-formats` style feature), the sub-feature is supported when the list contains it.
* If the feature isn't supported, or has no array arguments, the result is `false`.
* Without the third parameter, behavior is unchanged.

Follow-up to #64156, which lets repeated `add_post_type_support()` calls merge their array arguments instead of overwriting them.

With that in place, `remove_post_type_support()` is the odd one out. It can only remove a feature as a whole. The `editor` feature now has several sub-features (`default-mode`, `notes`, ...), so turning off just one of them means removing the whole feature and registering it again with everything you want to keep:

{{{#!php
<?php
add_post_type_support( 'custom', 'editor', array(
    'default-mode' => 'template-locked',
    'notes'        => true,
) );

// Today: to drop only 'notes'.
remove_post_type_support( 'custom', 'editor' );
add_post_type_support( 'custom', 'editor', array(
    'default-mode' => 'template-locked',
) );
}}}

This was raised by @wildworks on the #64156 PR.

== Proposal

Add an optional third parameter, `$sub_features` (string or array of strings):

{{{#!php
<?php
remove_post_type_support( 'custom', 'editor', 'notes' );
remove_post_type_support( 'custom', 'editor', array( 'notes', 'default-mode' ) );
}}}

* For associative arguments, the given keys are removed regardless of their value.
* For list arguments (e.g. a `post-formats` style feature), matching values are removed and the list is reindexed.
* The feature itself stays supported, even when all of its sub-features are removed.
* If the feature has no array arguments, or isn't registered, nothing happens.
* Without the third parameter, behavior is unchanged and the whole feature is removed.

Checking for individual sub-features in `post_type_supports()` (also brought up on #64156) is left for a separate ticket.

Recent Composer versions deprecate the `config.audit.*` keys in favor of `config.policy`. Editors that validate `composer.json` against the current Composer schema (such as VS Code) now show this warning on the `audit` block:

{{{
DEPRECATED: use 'config.policy' instead. All 'audit.*' keys still work as a fallback for now. See https://getcomposer.org/doc/06-config.md#policy
}}}

The current configuration:

{{{
"audit": { "ignore": [ "GHSA-hmqg-cxww-wqhq", "PKSA-rdkp-vv9z-mjkg" ] },
}}}

The equivalent using `config.policy`:

{{{
"policy": {
      "advisories": {
              "ignore-id": [ "GHSA-hmqg-cxww-wqhq", "PKSA-rdkp-vv9z-mjkg" ]
      }
},
}}}

The legacy `audit.*` keys still work as a fallback, so nothing is broken today. Migrating now removes the warning and avoids breakage when Composer drops the fallback.

WordPress’ Shortcode system has been gradually de-ephasized, but remains in use by many plugins, themes, and Core itself. There are many ways that it is ripe for updating, particularly in harmony with the HTML API.

 - It needs a performance overhaul to replace memory-heavy and inefficient processor.
 - It needs to harmonize with HTML parsing now that WordPress has the ability to understand HTML content.
 - Ambiguities in the grammar needs better explicit documentation. These ambiguities are results of the PCRE patterns which //define// how Shortcodes are parsed.
 - The multiple ways of finding and processing Shortcodes need to be combined into a convenient and single semantic API.

This is a tracking issue to organize the long-term project.

== Notes

=== Parsing is extremely dynamic.

The parsing syntax for Shortcodes is itself dependent on the runtime system when the request is run and when functions like `do_shortcodes()` is called. There may be historic conflation between “only process registered shortcodes” and “only recognize syntax based on registered shortcodes.” This is because //only registered Shortcodes are recognized as Shortcode syntax//.

{{{#!php
<?php

add_shortcode( 's0', fn () => '' );
echo do_shortcodes( 'a[s0]b[s1]c' );
// 'ab[s1]c'
}}}

This dynamism can create surprising behavior, for example, because escaped Shortcodes are not recognized for non-registered Shortcodes. In the following snippet, one //might// expect that `[s0]` should not be rendered because it appears to be part of the content of the non-registered `[s1]` Shortcode, which itself is escaped, meaning that the entire input string would come out unaltered by `do_shortcodes()`.

{{{#!php
<?php

add_shortcode( 's0', fn () => '' );
echo do_shortcodes( '[[s1]a[s0]b[/s1]]' );
// '[[s1]ab[/s1]]'
}}}

This dynamism also implies that one cannot “find” the Shortcodes in an HTML document in isolation. In fact, a given document may have a different set of Shortcodes in it based on who is asking, when, and proverbially what direction the wind is blowing.

=== Parsing only matches positive assertive patterns.

Due to the way that Shortcode handling is based on PCRE patterns and functions, there is no inherent error-handling for malformed Shortcode syntax. Added to this, the matcher is written as a single PCRE pattern with several noteworthy characteristics;

 - Escaping brackets are captured on either side, regardless if they are balanced. Shortcodes which were meant to be escaped can be swallowed because of a missing escaping bracket at close.
 - Whether a Shortcode expects a closing tag is based on whether the closing tag appears in the same document. Non-registered Shortcodes can interact unexpectedly with closing tags, creating them when they were not intended to exist.
 - Rules for parsing switch between affirmative lists (e.g. “a tag name may contain these characters”) and negative lists (e.g. “to parse a tag name, match until these characters appear”) and this leaves room for different code to have different understandings of where Shortcodes exist.
 - Shortcodes without closing tags, or Shortcodes whose closing tags appear far later in a document, cause an undue performance burden during parsing.

{{{#!php
<?php
$input = 'a[s0]b[s1]c[/s0]d[/s1]e';
add_shortcode( 's1', fn ( $attrs, $content ) => "[s1:{$content}]" );
do_shortcodes( $input );
add_shortcode( 's0', fn ( $attrs, $content ) => "[s0:{$content}]" );
do_shortcodes( $input );

//   input: a[s0]b[s1]c[/s0]d[/s1]e
//      s1: a[s0]b[s1:c[/s0]d]e
// s0 + s1: a[s0:b[s1]c]d[/s1]e
}}}


The documentation thankfully notes some of the wishy-washy nature of Shortcode handling.

 - [https://codex.wordpress.org/Shortcode_API Shortcode API]
 - [https://codex.wordpress.org/Shortcode Shortcode]

This helpfully acknowledges that defining behavior is difficult given that most of the existing behaviors are emergency due to the way the PCRE pattern happened to have been written, but it misses out on being helpful in clarifying expectations for how given Shortcode syntax will be handled. It fails to build an intuition on Shortcode handling.

=== Behavior has changed over time.

There have been changes in how Shortcodes are processed and the documentation makes clear that not all existing behaviors are guaranteed. Almost all variation occurs in abnormal cases, where markup is malformed or where there is valid-looking Shortcode syntax for non-registered Shortcodes.

Notably, an attempt was made to only parse Shortcodes within HTML syntax tokens. Unfortunately, at the time this appeared, WordPress had no reliable mechanism for parsing HTML and so a number of extant behaviors remain as emergent from those decisions.

Enforcing a new intentional set of behaviors can clarify and simplify how Shortcodes work in WordPress.

=== Finding potential Shortcode closers first be much more efficient.

Shortcode closing tags are only matched with very specific syntax. Yet, when reaching an opening tag, it’s required to scan, potentially, to the end of the document to find out //if// a closing tag exists. This occurs for every opening Shortcode tag.

However, a quick pass through the document can find a set of candidate Shortcode closing tags, and as opening tags are found, a rapid search through this candidate list can return matching closers. Advancing past a candidate closing Shortcode purges the set of candidates, maintaining search efficiency.

== Tasks

 - [ ] Replace call to `wp_kses_hair_parse()` with HTML API parsing.
 - [ ] Rebuild parser with efficient streaming parser.
 - [ ] Define changes in behavior to normalize processing.

== Related work

 - #50683 Rebuild the Shortcode parser.

The Style Engine keeps a declaration's value twice: once as the value it was given, and once as the CSS it compiles. The two disagree when the value contains a run of whitespace inside a quoted string, because every value is filtered through `wp_strip_all_tags( $value, true )`, whose second argument replaces each run of `\r`, `\n`, `\t` or space with a single space.

== Reproduction ==

A font family whose name contains two spaces, which needs nothing but core:

{{{#!php
<?php
$styles = wp_style_engine_get_styles(
    array( 'typography' => array( 'fontFamily' => '"My  Font", sans-serif' ) )
);

$styles['declarations']['font-family'];  // "My  Font", sans-serif
$styles['css'];                          // font-family:"My Font", sans-serif;
}}}

The serialized CSS no longer names exactly the family that was supplied, so it may match a different family or fall back. Nothing reports that the value was changed.

== Where it happens ==

`WP_Style_Engine_CSS_Declarations::filter_declaration()`:

{{{#!php
<?php
$filtered_value = wp_strip_all_tags( $value, true );
}}}

and in `wp_strip_all_tags()`:

{{{#!php
<?php
if ( $remove_breaks ) {
    $text = preg_replace( '/[\r\n\t ]+/', ' ', $text );
}

return trim( $text );
}}}

The helper's `$remove_breaks` parameter is documented as removing "left over line breaks and white space chars", so this behavior is consistent with its contract. It is unsafe for CSS values where whitespace inside a quoted string is data rather than formatting, and the Style Engine applies it to every declaration it writes.

== How this came up ==

While adding `font-variation-settings` in [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/13789 #13789] (Trac #66198). The [https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/typography/opentype/spec/dvaraxisreg OpenType Design-Variation Axis Tag Registry] defines an axis tag as four bytes beginning with a letter, and allows a tag with fewer than four letters or digits to be padded with trailing spaces, so `"abc "` and `"a   "` are valid tags. Accepting them produced this:

{{{
value given:   array( 'a   ' => 12 )
declarations:  font-variation-settings: "a   " 12
compiled CSS:  font-variation-settings: "a " 12
}}}

`"a "` is a different tag from `"a   "`, so the axis is not the one that was asked for.

That pull request now refuses a padded tag. That is a limit rather than a fix: it keeps the Style Engine from writing a tag other than the one requested, and it leaves WordPress unable to carry a space-padded axis tag at all. The tags in ordinary use are four characters, so the practical cost is small, but the reason it was chosen is this ticket.

== Scope ==

This is not specific to typography. Any CSS value whose meaning depends on repeated whitespace inside a quoted string is affected, and the family-name case above needs no new feature to reproduce.

I am not proposing a particular fix here, because the call sits on the path of every declaration the Style Engine emits: narrowing it changes output well beyond typography, and it is part of a sanitization path, so it deserves to be looked at with that in mind rather than patched from inside a feature branch.

Related: Trac #66198, [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/13789 wordpress-develop#13789].

When Twenty Fourteen's Featured Content option "Don't display tag on front end" is enabled, the featured tag is still displayed in a post's tag list on the front end (e.g. the tag links under a single post).

=== Steps to Reproduce
1. Activate Twenty Fourteen.
2. Appearance > Customize > Featured Content: set the tag name to `featured` and enable "Don't display tag on front end".
3. Create a post with the tags `featured` and another tag, then publish.
4. View the post on the front end.

=== Expected Results
The `featured` tag is hidden from the post's tag list.

=== Actual Results
The `featured` tag is shown in the post's tag list.

=== Cause
`Featured_Content::hide_the_featured_term()` (filter on `get_the_terms`) removes the matching term with:

{{{
unset( $terms[ $term->term_id ] );
}}}

The array returned by `get_the_terms()` is indexed from 0, not keyed by term ID, so the featured tag stays in the list. Worse, if the post has more tags than the featured tag's ID, a different, unrelated tag is removed instead (e.g. featured tag ID 3 on a post with 4+ tags hides the 4th tag).

The sibling method `hide_featured_term()` (filter on `get_terms`) already uses the loop index correctly:

{{{
unset( $terms[ $order ] );
}}}

=== Proposed Fix
Use the loop index in `hide_the_featured_term()` as well:

{{{
unset( $terms[ $order ] );
}}}

I tested this locally (WordPress 7.2-alpha-63166-src, PHP 8.3.33): with the fix, the featured tag is hidden on the front end when the option is enabled, and still shown when the option is disabled.

Found while testing #64882 (a different issue in the same file). I'll submit a PR.

When using a translated version of WordPress (e.g., fr_FR) with the Classic Editor, the translated strings for the "Save Draft" and "Preview" buttons are longer than in English. This causes the buttons to wrap onto two lines within the #minor-publishing-actions container. 

Due to recent CSS changes applied to buttons, there is currently no vertical margin between them when they stack, causing a visual overlap.

== Steps to reproduce ==
1. Install and activate the Classic Editor plugin.
2. Set the Site Language to French (fr_FR) to ensure the button text is long enough to wrap.
3. Navigate to Articles → Ajouter un article.
4. Observe the "Publier" metabox (specifically the "Enregistrer le brouillon" and "Prévisualiser" buttons).
5. Notice that the buttons are stacked and touch each other without any vertical spacing.

== Expected result ==
There should be a consistent vertical gap between the buttons when they are forced onto multiple lines.

== Possible solution ==
A simple CSS adjustment could be added to handle the spacing when items wrap. For example, applying a bottom margin to the first button's container or utilizing flexbox gap on the parent #minor-publishing-actions:

{{{
#minor-publishing-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 8px;
    justify-content: space-between;
}

/* Fallback/alternative */
#save-post {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
}
}}}

When a `$path` is passed to `wp_get_global_settings()` or `wp_get_global_styles()` and that path does not exist, both functions return the '''entire''' settings/styles array instead of an empty value.

This makes it impossible for callers to distinguish "this setting is not set" from "here is every setting", so defensive code has to sniff for expected keys and guess.

Originally reported in Gutenberg: https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/issues/39657

== Steps to reproduce ==

{{{#!php
<?php
add_action( 'init', function () {
	var_dump( wp_get_global_settings( array( 'layout', 'contentSize' ) ) ); // string, e.g. '645px'
	var_dump( wp_get_global_settings( array( 'foo', 'bar' ) ) );            // entire settings array (12 top-level keys)
	var_dump( wp_get_global_styles( array( 'foo', 'bar' ) ) );              // entire styles array
}, 9999 );
}}}

== Expected ==

`null` when the requested path does not exist.

== Actual ==

The full settings (or styles) array.

== Cause ==

In `src/wp-includes/global-styles-and-settings.php`:

{{{#!php
return _wp_array_get( $settings, $path, $settings ); // wp_get_global_settings()
return _wp_array_get( $styles, $path, $styles );     // wp_get_global_styles()
}}}

The third argument of `_wp_array_get()` is the default returned when the path is not found, and here it is the whole array.

== Proposed fix ==

Return `null` for a missing path (i.e. drop the third argument / pass `null`), while keeping the existing behaviour of returning everything when `$path` is empty:

{{{#!php
if ( empty( $path ) ) {
	return $settings;
}
return _wp_array_get( $settings, $path, null );
}}}

Why `null` rather than an empty array:
 * It is `_wp_array_get()`'s own default for a missing path.
 * It distinguishes "not set" from a setting whose value legitimately ''is'' an empty array.
 * It makes the `?? $fallback` idiom work for callers.

== Backward compatibility ==

This is a behaviour change. Callers of both functions should be audited for any that pass a path that may be absent. In practice, a caller currently receiving the full array for a missing path is already getting an unusable value, so impact should be minimal. A dev note may be warranted.

Also needs unit tests covering: existing path, missing path, and empty path (returns full array).

`wp_nav_menu()` passes the result of `wp_get_nav_menu_items()` to `_wp_menu_item_classes_by_context()` ( [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/trunk/src/wp-includes/nav-menu-template.php#L201 GH] ), which assigns properties on every item without checking that the item is an object ( [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/trunk/src/wp-includes/nav-menu-template.php#L399 GH] ). The items come through the `wp_get_nav_menu_items` filter, and in the normal case they are all WP_Post objects.

A filter that leaves a `null` in that array causes a PHP fatal error:

{{{
Fatal error: Uncaught Error: Attempt to assign property "current" on null ...
}}}

WordPress should check that each menu item is an object before assigning properties to it.

I have a PR to address this.

